  "segment": "Investing",
  "h1": "Bonds vs Bond Funds for Your Portfolio and Timeline",
  "introText": "Individual bonds fit a known date, while a bond fund fits diversified, ongoing buying. If you buy an individual bond and hold it until its maturity date, the borrower fulfills its debt obligation by paying you the final interest payment and the face value of the bond, called par value, unless the issuer defaults or calls the bond early. Most bond funds have no set maturity date. Instead of waiting for a principal repayment date, a fund investor holds shares in a pooled portfolio and redeems them at the next calculated net asset value (NAV), minus any redemption fees.\n\nRising interest rates push bond prices down across the entire fixed-income market, which means both individual bonds and bond funds lose market value when prevailing rates increase. A bondholder who holds an individual security to maturity avoids secondary-market price losses, but faces credit risk, inflation risk, and call risk. When you buy an individual bond from a brokerage firm acting as a principal, the firm may add a markup to your purchase price or apply a markdown if you sell. Bond funds spread risk across a range of companies and industries, yet they charge fees and expenses that reduce your returns over time.",
  "tableHeading": "Bonds vs Bond Funds at a Glance",
